cation-triggered 39.2% vs random 31.8% vs no-change 34.1%. p=0.042.\n\n**Table 10 (B-IAVR training):** Converges to 31.3% success, 71.2% verify rate at step 18.\n\n**Table 11 (Full comparison):** B-IAVR 31.3%/71.2% vs confidence-threshold 30.5%/62.4% vs random-50% 28.1%/50% vs no-verify 23.4%.\n\n**Table 12 (Reward ablation):** Action-change 31.3%/22.1% cost reduction vs confidence-based 30.1%/24.8% vs random 27.8%/31.4%.\n\n**Table 13 (ARES comparison):** B-IAVR 31.3%/22.1% cost reduction vs ARES 29.7%/33.8%.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiment Coverage)\n\nThe review_context.yaml lists 15 planned experiments. The paper covers all of them. Key concerns from context:\n- h-sweep has noise (not perfectly monotonic across seeds)\n- Multi-seed std=4.7pp (moderate variance)\n- Action-change correlation weak (r=0.19 raw, r=0.14 partial)\n- B-IAVR selective verification effect not dramatic (only 22% cost reduction, 4.6pp success loss)\n- B-IAVR barely beats confidence-threshold (+0.8pp)\n- ARES comparison is mixed (ARES gets better cost reduction)\n- Causal intervention p=0.042 barely significant\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Missing Experiments / Analysis**\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on methodological soundness and experimental design.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MrfiGHVkGMvLkExWQg5WRx","session_id":"10a3f31b-71c7-4a9c-9f06-cb705222db6e","uuid":"567a9772-0a14-4949-bf4f-e8b7edfed4ab","timestamp":"2026-05-17T00:21:29.276Z"}
