7% on ALFWorld with Qwen-1.7B, always-on), ReflAct (GPT-4), Inner Monologue.\n3. **Adaptive Computation and Selective Reasoning**: ARES (SFT router, 52.7% token reduction), Thinkless (RL for reasoning tokens, single-turn).\n\nOnly 10 references total.\n\n## Key Results for Novelty Assessment\n\n- Episode-level vs step-independent: 34.9% vs 0% (dramatic gap)\n- B-IAVR vs confidence-threshold: 31.3% vs 30.5% (marginal, +0.8pp)\n- B-IAVR vs ARES: 31.3%/22.1% cost reduction vs 29.7%/33.8% (mixed, neither dominates)\n- Action-change signal: r=0.14 after entropy control (weak)\n- Causal evidence: p=0.042 (barely significant)\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Novelty Assessment** (is each contribution genuinely new? is the combination sufficient?)\n3. **Related Work Gaps** (missing citations, positioning issues)\n4. **Framing and Writing Quality** (is the story coherent? are claims calibrated to evidence?)\n5. **Strengths** (bulleted)\n6. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with severity)\n7. **Questions for Authors** (3-5)\n8. **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nKey novelty questions to consider:\n- Is \"episode-level reward attribution\" genuinely novel, or is it just a standard REINFORCE-style approach that has been known for decades?\n- The credit assignment decay analysis (γ^(T-h)) is mathematically obvious once stated. Is characterizing it empirically sufficient contribution?\n- B-IAVR results are weak. Does the paper try to package a negative result as positive?\n- Self-Guide already achieves 72.7% on ALFWorld with Qwen-1.7B. This paper's episode-level training achieves 34.9%. How should this gap be interpreted?\n- Only 10 references. Is this sufficient for a venue paper?\n\nBe rigorous. Focus on whether the novelty bar is met for a top ML venue.\n","uuid":"bc0d4e28-b8df-4627-9737-e62d7597360e","session_id":"10a3f31b-71c7-4a9c-9f06-cb705222db6e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate originality, positioning relative to prior art, whether the contribution is sufficient for a top venue, and framing/writing quality.\n\nUse a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Episode-Level Credit Assignment for Multi-Turn Agent Training: Learning When to Verify via Reinforcement Learning\"\n\nThe paper makes two contributions:\n1. **Episode-level GRPO** resolves credit assignment collapse in multi-turn agent RL. Step-independent training achieves 0% on ALFWorld; episode-level achieves 34.9±4.7%. The credit assignment failure is characterized via h-sweep (h∈{2,5,10,20,full}), gradient signal analysis, ScienceWorld transfer, and 7B scaling.\n2. **B-IAVR** (Binary Informativeness-Aware Verification by RL): trains a selective verification policy using action-change as intrinsic reward. Reduces verification cost by 22% with 4.6pp success trade-off. Marginally outperforms random-50%, roughly matches confidence-threshold baseline.\n\n## Method Details\n\n- Episode-level GRPO: attributes episode reward R equally to all steps. Policy gradient uses batch-normalized rewards. Eliminates discount-based decay.\n- B-IAVR reward: R = R_task + α·Σ(action_changed when verified) - β·Σ(verify count). α=0.5, β=0.1.\n- Base model: Qwen3-1.7B. Environments: ALFWorld (primary), ScienceWorld (transfer). Also tested Qwen3-7B.\n\n## Related Work as Written in Paper\n\nThree paragraphs:\n1. **RL for LM Agents**: RAGEN, AgentTrek, WebRL. Claims these systems assume per-step credit assignment is sufficient.\n2. **Self-Verification and Reflection**: Self-Guide (72.7% on ALFWorld with Qwen-1.7B, always-on), ReflAct (GPT-4), Inner Monologue.\n3. **Adaptive Computation and Selective Reasoning**: ARES (SFT router, 52.7% token reduction), Thinkless (RL for reasoning tokens, single-turn).\n\nOnly 10 references total.\n\n## Key Results for Novelty Assessment\n\n- Episode-level vs step-independent: 34.9% vs 0% (dramatic gap)\n- B-IAVR vs confidence-threshold: 31.3% vs 30.5% (marginal, +0.8pp)\n- B-IAVR vs ARES: 31.3%/22.1% cost reduction vs 29.7%/33.8% (mixed, neither dominates)\n- Action-change signal: r=0.14 after entropy control (weak)\n- Causal evidence: p=0.042 (barely significant)\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Novelty Assessment** (is each contribution genuinely new? is the combination sufficient?)\n3. **Related Work Gaps** (missing citations, positioning issues)\n4. **Framing and Writing Quality** (is the story coherent? are claims calibrated to evidence?)\n5. **Strengths** (bulleted)\n6. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with severity)\n7. **Questions for Authors** (3-5)\n8. **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nKey novelty questions to consider:\n- Is \"episode-level reward attribution\" genuinely novel, or is it just a standard REINFORCE-style approach that has been known for decades?\n- The credit assignment decay analysis (γ^(T-h)) is mathematically obvious once stated. Is characterizing it empirically sufficient contribution?\n- B-IAVR results are weak. Does the paper try to package a negative result as positive?\n- Self-Guide already achieves 72.7% on ALFWorld with Qwen-1.7B. This paper's episode-level training achieves 34.9%. How should this gap be interpreted?\n- Only 10 references. Is this sufficient for a venue paper?\n\nBe rigorous. Focus on whether the novelty bar is met for a top ML venue.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014YZHjSxD3JEcHcwf2fqBic","session_id":"10a3f31b-71c7-4a9c-9f06-cb705222db6e","uuid":"c6a7603e-b359-4af8-9ac6-2ce189a6057a","timestamp":"2026-05-17T00:21:50.997Z"}
