{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NUXszzGPVxEJcWuYLDGkkg","type":"tool_result","content":"20\tReinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a promising approach to train language model agents for multi-turn interactive tasks. However, existing frameworks such as RAGEN default to step-independent reward assignment, where each action receives credit based only on its local context window. We identify a fundamental failure mode of this approach: in tasks requiring tens of interaction steps, the effective discount factor $\\gamma^{T-h}$ decays to near zero for early actions, causing catastrophic training collapse. On ALFWorld with a mean episode length of 48 steps and $\\gamma=0.99$, step-independent training ($h=1$) yields $\\gamma^{47} \\approx 0.006$, reducing gradient signal to 0.6\\% of its full-episode magnitude. We propose episode-level Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), which assigns rewards using the complete trajectory. Experiments on ALFWorld and ScienceWorld with Qwen3-1.7B show that episode-level training achieves 24.3\\% $\\pm$ 6.0\\% success versus 0\\% for step-independent training (complete collapse). A context window sweep across $h \\in \\{1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 10, 15, 20, \\text{full}\\}$ reveals a sharp performance cliff below $h=5$, consistent with the predicted discount decay. The advantage generalizes to ScienceWorld (15\\% vs.\\ 2\\%) and scales to Qwen3-7B (38\\% vs.\\ 5\\%), confirming that the credit assignment bottleneck is environment- and scale-independent.\n21\t\\end{abstract}\n22\t\n23\t\\section{Introduction}\n24\t\n25\tTraining language model agents to operate in multi-turn interactive environments remains difficult. While single-turn tasks benefit from straightforward reward-to-action attribution, multi-turn settings require agents to execute sequences of 30--60 actions before receiving a terminal reward signal. Recent work on RL for language agents, including RAGEN and similar GRPO-based frameworks, has adopted step-independent training: each action is scored within a local context window of size $h$, effectively treating a multi-step episode as a collection of independent single-step decisions.\n26\t\n27\tThis design choice introduces a fundamental problem. When a terminal reward $R$ is assigned to an action $h$ steps before the end of an episode of length $T$, the effective credit assignment signal decays as $\\gamma^{T-h}$. For step-independent training where $h=1$, the first action in an episode receives a gradient signal proportional to $\\gamma^{T-1}$. With $\\gamma=0.99$ and $T=48$ (the mean episode length in ALFWorld), this equals $0.99^{47} \\approx 0.006$, reducing the training signal to 0.6\\% of full magnitude. Actions in the first half of an episode receive effectively zero credit, making coherent multi-step planning unlearnable.\n28\t\n29\tWe present a systematic study of this failure mode and its resolution. Our key contributions are:\n30\t\n31\t\\begin{enumerate}\n32\t\\item We identify credit assignment decay as the root cause of training collapse in step-independent RL for multi-turn agents, providing both theoretical analysis ($\\gamma^{T-h}$ decay characterization) and empirical evidence (complete collapse at $h \\leq 2$).\n33\t\n34\t\\item We demonstrate that episode-level GRPO, which uses the full trajectory for reward assignment, eliminates this bottleneck, achieving 24.3\\% $\\pm$ 6.0\\% success on ALFWorld where step-independent training achieves 0\\%.\n35\t\n36\t\\item We conduct a fine-grained context window sweep ($h \\in \\{1,2,3,5,8,10,15,20,\\text{full}\\}$) revealing a sharp performance cliff below $h=5$, with success rate monotonically increasing as $h$ grows. This provides practitioners with actionable guidance on minimum context requirements.\n37\t\n38\t\\item We validate generalization across environments (ALFWorld and ScienceWorld) and model scales (1.7B and 7B parameters), showing the advantage is robust and scale-independent.\n39\t\\end{enumerate}\n40\t\n41\t\\section{Related Work}\n42\t\n43\t\\paragraph{RL for Language Model Agents.}\n44\tRAGEN introduced GRPO-based training for interactive agents but employs step-independent reward assignment as its default configuration. AgentQ and similar approaches use per-step value estimation, inheriting the same credit assignment limitations in long-horizon settings. Our work identifies why these approaches struggle on tasks exceeding 30 steps and proposes episode-level training as a fix.\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{Multi-Turn Agent Frameworks.}\n47\tSelf-Guide demonstrated that self-verification improves interactive agents, achieving 72.7\\% on ALFWorld with Qwen-1.7B using prompted verification at every step. ReflAct uses GPT-4 level models with reflection prompting. ARES trains an SFT-based router for adaptive verification effort. These works focus on inference-time strategies; our contribution addresses the orthogonal training-time bottleneck.\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Credit Assignment in RL.}\n50\tThe credit assignment problem is classical in RL. Temporal difference methods, eligibility traces, and hindsight experience replay address aspects of this challenge. In the LLM agent setting, the interaction between discount-based credit decay and extremely long episodes (30--60 steps of multi-token actions) creates a regime where standard approaches catastrophically fail. Our analysis quantifies this regime-specific failure.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Adaptive Computation for LLM Agents.}\n53\tThinkless trains RL-based control tokens to decide reasoning depth. This shares our philosophy of using RL to control agent computation, applied to a different dimension (reasoning depth vs.\\ verification frequency). Our training methodology (episode-level GRPO) could serve as the backbone for such approaches in multi-turn settings.\n54\t\n55\t\\section{Method}\n56\t\n57\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n58\t\n59\tConsider a multi-turn interactive agent operating in an environment for $T$ steps. At each step $t$, the agent observes state $s_t$, generates action $a_t$, and receives environment feedback. A terminal reward $R \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ is assigned at episode end based on task completion.\n60\t\n61\tIn step-independent training, each action $a_t$ is optimized using a context window of $h$ preceding steps. The effective gradient signal for action $a_t$ is proportional to:\n62\t\\begin{equation}\n63\tg_t \\propto \\gamma^{T - t} \\cdot R \\cdot \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t | s_{\\max(1, t-h):t})\n64\t\\end{equation}\n65\t\n66\tFor $h=1$ (single-step context) and early actions ($t \\ll T$), $\\gamma^{T-t}$ approaches zero, rendering learning impossible.\n67\t\n68\t\\subsection{Credit Assignment Decay Analysis}\n69\t\n70\tWe formalize the decay. Let $T$ denote mean episode length and $\\gamma$ the discount factor. The relative gradient signal for an action at position $h$ steps from episode end is:\n71\t\\begin{equation}\n72\t\\text{RelSignal}(h) = \\gamma^{T-h} / \\gamma^0 = \\gamma^{T-h}\n73\t\\end{equation}\n74\t\n75\tFor ALFWorld ($T=48$, $\\gamma=0.99$):\n76\t\\begin{itemize}\n77\t\\item $h=1$: RelSignal $= 0.99^{47} = 0.006$ (0.6\\% of full signal)\n78\t\\item $h=5$: RelSignal $= 0.99^{43} = 0.03$ (3\\%)\n79\t\\item $h=10$: RelSignal $= 0.99^{38} = 0.22$ (22\\%)\n80\t\\item $h=20$: RelSignal $= 0.99^{28} = 0.67$ (67\\%)\n81\t\\item $h=\\text{full}$: RelSignal $= 1.0$ (100\\%)\n82\t\\end{itemize}\n83\t\n84\tThis predicts a sharp transition: below approximately $h=10$, gradient signal drops below 25\\% of full magnitude; below $h=5$, it drops below 5\\%. Actions receiving less than 5\\% relative signal effectively receive no training pressure, leading to policy collapse into repetitive or invalid outputs.\n85\t\n86\t\\subsection{Episode-Level GRPO}\n87\t\n88\tWe propose using the full episode trajectory for reward assignment. In Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), a group of $G$ trajectories is sampled, and rewards are normalized within the group. Episode-level GRPO assigns the terminal reward $R$ to all actions in the trajectory equally:\n89\t\\begin{equation}\n90\t\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{ep-GRPO}} = -\\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\hat{A}_{\\text{group}}(R) \\cdot \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t | s_{1:t})\n91\t\\end{equation}\n92\twhere $\\hat{A}_{\\text{group}}(R) = (R - \\mu_G) / \\sigma_G$ is the group-normalized advantage. Every action receives full gradient signal regardless of position, eliminating the $\\gamma^{T-h}$ decay.\n93\t\n94\tThe key difference from step-independent GRPO: each action conditions on the full preceding context $s_{1:t}$ rather than a truncated window $s_{t-h:t}$, and receives reward credit proportional to the episode outcome rather than a discounted version.\n95\t\n96\t\\section{Experiments}\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Setup}\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Environments.} We evaluate on ALFWorld (household task completion, mean episode length 48 steps) and ScienceWorld (science experiment execution, mean 35 steps). Both require multi-step planning with sparse terminal rewards.\n101\t\n102\t\\paragraph{Model.} Qwen3-1.7B as primary model, with Qwen3-7B for scaling verification. Training uses GRPO with KL coefficient 0.01, group size 8, learning rate $5 \\times 10^{-6}$.\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} Step-independent GRPO ($h=1$) following RAGEN defaults. Additional context window sizes $h \\in \\{2, 3, 5, 8, 10, 15, 20\\}$ for ablation.\n105\t\n106\t\\subsection{Context Window Sweep (Core Result)}\n107\t\n108\tTable~\\ref{tab:hsweep} presents our primary result: success rate as a function of context window size $h$.\n109\t\n110\t\\begin{table}[h]\n111\t\\centering\n112\t\\caption{Context window ($h$) sweep on ALFWorld with Qwen3-1.7B. Step-independent training ($h \\leq 2$) causes complete collapse. Performance increases monotonically with $h$, with a sharp cliff below $h=5$.}\n113\t\\label{tab:hsweep}\n114\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n115\t\\toprule\n116\t$h$ & Success (\\%) & Action Valid (\\%) & Conv.\\ Step & $\\gamma^{T-h}$ (mean) \\\\\n117\t\\midrule\n118\t1 & 0.0 & 45.0 & collapse & 0.0001 \\\\\n119\t2 & 2.0 & 58.0 & near-collapse & 0.0008 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MrfiGHVkGMvLkExWQg5WRx","session_id":"10a3f31b-71c7-4a9c-9f06-cb705222db6e","uuid":"961a7ab9-cbf0-4bbc-83d2-372f5af38082","timestamp":"2026-05-17T00:21:59.632Z"}
