{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013h6WbH86AktqphfmwbmCAf","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tTo establish that verification-triggered changes are genuinely corrective (not random perturbation), we compare three conditions at matched change rates (Table~\\ref{tab:causal}). Verification-triggered changes yield 50.3\\% success vs.\\ 36.8\\% for random action changes ($p < 0.001$, $n = 320$ per condition), a 13.5pp gap confirming the causal corrective role of verification.\n201\t\n202\t\\begin{table}[h]\n203\t\\centering\n204\t\\caption{Causal intervention: verification-triggered vs.\\ random action changes at matched rate.}\n205\t\\label{tab:causal}\n206\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n207\t\\toprule\n208\tCondition & $n$ & Success (\\%) & 95\\% CI \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\tVerification-triggered & 320 & 50.3 & [46.1, 54.5] \\\\\n211\tRandom (matched rate) & 320 & 36.8 & [32.8, 40.8] \\\\\n212\tNo-change control & 320 & 44.1 & [39.9, 48.3] \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\end{table}\n216\t\n217\t\\subsection{Selective Verification Results}\n218\t\n219\t\\paragraph{B-IAVR Training.}\n220\tStarting from the converged always-on checkpoint (48.2\\% success), B-IAVR training converges in 18 steps (Table~\\ref{tab:biavr_training}). The model learns to skip confirmatory verifications while retaining corrective ones: when it chooses to verify, 58\\% of calls trigger action changes (vs.\\ 32\\% under always-on), indicating successful identification of high-uncertainty moments.\n221\t\n222\t\\begin{table}[h]\n223\t\\centering\n224\t\\caption{B-IAVR training progression from always-on checkpoint.}\n225\t\\label{tab:biavr_training}\n226\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n227\t\\toprule\n228\tStep & Success (\\%) & Verify Rate (\\%) & Action Change When Verified (\\%) & Cost Reduction (\\%) \\\\\n229\t\\midrule\n230\t0 & 48.2 & 100 & 32 & 0 \\\\\n231\t5 & 47.5 & 78 & 38 & 19 \\\\\n232\t10 & 47.1 & 63 & 47 & 33 \\\\\n233\t15 & 46.9 & 55 & 54 & 41 \\\\\n234\t18 & 46.8 & 52 & 58 & 43 \\\\\n235\t25 & 46.5 & 48 & 61 & 47 \\\\\n236\t30 & 45.9 & 44 & 63 & 51 \\\\\n237\t\\bottomrule\n238\t\\end{tabular}\n239\t\\end{table}\n240\t\n241\t\\paragraph{Full Comparison.}\n242\tTable~\\ref{tab:full_comparison} presents the main result. B-IAVR achieves 46.8\\% success with 52\\% verification rate, reducing cost by 43\\% at only 1.4pp success loss (within the 2.9pp multi-seed standard deviation). It outperforms confidence-threshold (+2.5pp), random-p(0.5) (+4.7pp), and no-verify (+11.2pp) baselines at matched or lower verification cost. B-IAVR is the only Pareto-optimal method: no other approach achieves equal success at equal or lower cost.\n243\t\n244\t\\begin{table}[h]\n245\t\\centering\n246\t\\caption{Full comparison of verification strategies on ALFWorld.}\n247\t\\label{tab:full_comparison}\n248\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n249\t\\toprule\n250\tMethod & Success (\\%) & Verify Rate (\\%) & Tokens & Efficiency (succ/tok$\\times$1000) \\\\\n251\t\\midrule\n252\tAlways-on & 48.2 & 100 & 128,400 & 0.375 \\\\\n253\t\\textbf{B-IAVR (ours)} & \\textbf{46.8} & \\textbf{52} & \\textbf{73,200} & \\textbf{0.639} \\\\\n254\tConfidence-threshold & 44.3 & 53 & 75,600 & 0.586 \\\\\n255\tRandom-p(0.5) & 42.1 & 50 & 74,800 & 0.563 \\\\\n256\tNo-verify & 35.6 & 0 & 52,100 & 0.683 \\\\\n257\t\\bottomrule\n258\t\\end{tabular}\n259\t\\end{table}\n260\t\n261\t\\paragraph{Per-Task Breakdown.}\n262\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_task} shows B-IAVR maintains performance uniformly across task types, with maximum per-task degradation of 2.3pp (pick\\_two), indicating the selective policy does not sacrifice specific task categories.\n263\t\n264\t\\begin{table}[h]\n265\t\\centering\n266\t\\caption{Per-task success breakdown across verification strategies.}\n267\t\\label{tab:per_task}\n268\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n269\t\\toprule\n270\tMethod & pick & clean & heat & cool & examine & pick\\_two \\\\\n271\t\\midrule\n272\tAlways-on & 56.2 & 48.7 & 52.1 & 45.3 & 62.5 & 28.4 \\\\\n273\tB-IAVR & 55.1 & 47.2 & 50.8 & 43.9 & 61.3 & 26.1 \\\\\n274\tNo-verify & 42.3 & 35.1 & 38.7 & 31.2 & 48.4 & 18.6 \\\\\n275\t\\bottomrule\n276\t\\end{tabular}\n277\t\\end{table}\n278\t\n279\t\\subsection{Reward Signal Ablation}\n280\t\n281\tTable~\\ref{tab:reward_ablation} compares four reward formulations for training the selective policy. Action-change reward achieves the highest success at matched cost, outperforming confidence-based (+1.7pp), task-success-only (+2.0pp), and random-trigger (+3.1pp). The advantage stems from action-change being simultaneously dense (per-verification) and informative (directly measures utility), while alternatives sacrifice one dimension.\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[h]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\caption{Reward signal ablation for selective verification training.}\n286\t\\label{tab:reward_ablation}\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\tReward Type & Success (\\%) & Verify Rate (\\%) & Cost Reduction (\\%) & Efficiency \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tAction-change (ours) & 46.8 & 52 & 43 & 0.639 \\\\\n292\tConfidence-based & 45.1 & 54 & 41 & 0.601 \\\\\n293\tTask-success-only & 44.8 & 38 & 56 & 0.617 \\\\\n294\tRandom-trigger & 43.7 & 51 & 44 & 0.581 \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\end{table}\n298\t\n299\t\\subsection{Comparison with ARES}\n300\t\n301\tTable~\\ref{tab:ares} provides head-to-head comparison with a reproduced ARES supervised router. B-IAVR achieves 9pp greater cost reduction and 1.9pp higher success without requiring any labeled training data (ARES requires 5,000 oracle-labeled examples). The RL policy explores more aggressive verification skipping strategies than SFT can learn from demonstrations.\n302\t\n303\t\\begin{table}[h]\n304\t\\centering\n305\t\\caption{B-IAVR vs.\\ reproduced ARES on ALFWorld.}\n306\t\\label{tab:ares}\n307\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n308\t\\toprule\n309\tMetric & B-IAVR (ours) & ARES (reproduced) & Advantage \\\\\n310\t\\midrule\n311\tSuccess (\\%) & 46.8 & 44.9 & +1.9 \\\\\n312\tVerify Rate (\\%) & 52 & 61 & $-$9 (better) \\\\\n313\tCost Reduction (\\%) & 43 & 34 & +9 \\\\\n314\tTraining Labels & 0 & 5,000 & Self-supervised \\\\\n315\tGPU Hours & 96 & 24 & $-$72 (trade-off) \\\\\n316\t\\bottomrule\n317\t\\end{tabular}\n318\t\\end{table}\n319\t\n320\t\\subsection{Generalization}\n321\t\n322\t\\paragraph{Cross-Environment.}\n323\tTable~\\ref{tab:generalization} shows episode-level training generalizes to ScienceWorld (mean $T = 67$), achieving 32.4\\% success vs.\\ 4.8\\% for step-independent (6.75$\\times$ improvement). The longer episodes in ScienceWorld make credit assignment decay even more severe, consistent with our mechanistic analysis.\n324\t\n325\t\\paragraph{Model Scale.}\n326\tQwen3-7B achieves 58.7\\% with episode-level training vs.\\ 8.3\\% step-independent (7.1$\\times$). The advantage persists across scale, with 7B converging faster (8 vs.\\ 12 steps) due to stronger initialization but maintaining comparable improvement ratios.\n327\t\n328\t\\begin{table}[h]\n329\t\\centering\n330\t\\caption{Generalization across environments and model scales.}\n331\t\\label{tab:generalization}\n332\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n333\t\\toprule\n334\tSetting & Episode-Level (\\%) & Step-Independent (\\%) & Ratio & Conv.\\ Step \\\\\n335\t\\midrule\n336\tALFWorld / 1.7B & 48.2 & 6.0 & 8.0$\\times$ & 12 \\\\\n337\tALFWorld / 7B & 58.7 & 8.3 & 7.1$\\times$ & 8 \\\\\n338\tScienceWorld / 1.7B & 32.4 & 4.8 & 6.75$\\times$ & 20 \\\\\n339\t\\bottomrule\n340\t\\end{tabular}\n341\t\\end{table}\n342\t\n343\t\\subsection{Per-Task Analysis}\n344\t\n345\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_task_detail} reveals that episode-level advantage correlates with episode length ($r = 0.61$): longer tasks like pick\\_two ($T = 48.3$, 9.5$\\times$ advantage) benefit more than shorter tasks like examine ($T = 18.7$, 5.2$\\times$ advantage). This confirms that credit assignment decay severity scales with horizon length, as predicted by the $\\gamma^{T-h}$ mechanism.\n346\t\n347\t\\begin{table}[h]\n348\t\\centering\n349\t\\caption{Per-task performance with episode length and relative advantage over step-independent.}\n350\t\\label{tab:per_task_detail}\n351\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n352\t\\toprule\n353\tTask Type & Success (\\%) & Mean Episode Length & Ep.-Level Advantage \\\\\n354\t\\midrule\n355\texamine & 62.5 & 18.7 & 5.2$\\times$ \\\\\n356\tpick & 56.2 & 22.4 & 6.8$\\times$ \\\\\n357\theat & 52.1 & 31.2 & 7.9$\\times$ \\\\\n358\tclean & 48.7 & 35.6 & 8.3$\\times$ \\\\\n359\tcool & 45.3 & 33.8 & 8.1$\\times$ \\\\\n360\tpick\\_two & 28.4 & 48.3 & 9.5$\\times$ \\\\\n361\t\\bottomrule\n362\t\\end{tabular}\n363\t\\end{table}\n364\t\n365\t\\subsection{No-Verify Ablation}\n366\t\n367\tTable~\\ref{tab:no_verify} isolates verification's contribution. Removing verification from the episode-level agent drops success by 12.6pp (48.2\\% to 35.6\\%), confirming that verification provides genuine value rather than mere overhead. The gap is largest for complex tasks requiring navigation correction, motivating selective rather than eliminated verification.\n368\t\n369\t\\begin{table}[h]\n370\t\\centering\n371\t\\caption{No-verify ablation: episode-level agent with and without verification.}\n372\t\\label{tab:no_verify}\n373\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n374\t\\toprule\n375\tMetric & No-Verify & Always-On & Delta \\\\\n376\t\\midrule\n377\tSuccess (\\%) & 35.6 & 48.2 & $-$12.6 \\\\\n378\tValid Actions (\\%) & 91.2 & 94.8 & $-$3.6 \\\\\n379\tMean Episode Length & 44.8 & 28.4 & +16.4 \\\\\n380\tConvergence Step & 15 & 12 & +3 \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table}\n384\t\n385\t\\section{Conclusion}\n386\t\n387\tWe presented a two-part framework for efficient multi-turn agent training and deployment. Episode-level GRPO resolves credit assignment decay that makes step-independent RL catastrophically fail on long-horizon tasks, achieving 48.2\\% success on ALFWorld (8$\\times$ over baselines). Built on this foundation, B-IAVR learns when verification is informative using action-change as a self-supervised reward, reducing verification cost by 48\\% with negligible performance loss. The dual contribution is mutually dependent: without episode-level training there is no sufficiently capable agent to verify selectively; without selective verification, episode-level training yields a capable but computationally expensive system. Results generalize across environments, model scales, and random seeds.\n388\t\n389\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n390\tExperiments are conducted on text-based environments with discrete action spaces. Extension to continuous-action or vision-grounded settings remains future work. B-IAVR training requires a pre-converged episode-level checkpoint, adding to total GPU cost (96h vs.\\ 24h for supervised alternatives).\n391\t\n392\t\\end{document}\n393\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014YZHjSxD3JEcHcwf2fqBic","session_id":"10a3f31b-71c7-4a9c-9f06-cb705222db6e","uuid":"5ad02d66-18f6-4210-9c7f-d73aab135905","timestamp":"2026-05-17T00:22:20.265Z"}
